  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Mechanism
    
    Threk's Opening is a war-ritual technique developed during the Orca Incursion that channels pressure-depth magic through the practitioner's skeleton. It is not taught but *opened*: ritual scarification along the spine and ribs creates channels for abyssal force drawn from the Berg's deepest trenches. When invoked, the body becomes a conduit — bones resonate with the weight of fathoms, joints lock into geometries that should not articulate, and the water around the practitioner moves in ways I lack the hydrodynamic vocabulary to describe precisely. Strikes delivered through the Opening carry crushing deep-current force sufficient to shatter ice-armor or snap a penguin's wing-bone clean.
    
    The technique subsumed the Grend Blood-Arts, the older orca predatory doctrine binding blood-sense, pressure-strike, and deep-communion into coordinated pod action. What replaced it was cruder and more efficient — a weapon built for an incursion, not a tradition.
    
    ## Threk Boneday and Its Cost
    
    Thung Thrakrek, the scarred orca war-leader, awakened the Opening during Threk Boneday, a massed ritual in which shaman-pods cracked the Berg's oldest ice to bind pod-strength into a single synchronized assault. Three shaman-pods lost their minds. The binding was designed to last one day and die with its channelers. It did not.
    
    The physical cost to practitioners is documented and consistent across sources: permanent stiffness, fin tremors, teeth cracked from pressure-vibration. Thrakrek bore these marks visibly, though those who trained under him noted he flinched when the cold deepened.
    
    ## Persistence
    
    Bone struck by Threk's Opening retains residual vibration. Fractured penguin wing-bones recovered from Incursion-era engagements still produce anomalous resonance — a fact confirmed by interrogation of blood-speaker initiate Kzund-threk-zaith, who attested the binding persisted beyond its intended duration. Whether it can be reopened without the original shaman-pods remains unsettled among orca war-councils. The ice-memory evidence I have examined is consistent with an active, if attenuated, signature.
