  annotation "note_1771585993903_0", anchor: "a weapon built for an incursion, not a tradition", text: "The Grend Blood-Arts contained penguin communion techniques folded into orca killing doctrine — I documented this in that entry and will not repeat the argument here. When Threk's Opening consumed the Blood-Arts, it consumed that inheritance too. There is penguin pressure-magic in the skeleton of every orca who channels the Opening. Neither side's chroniclers acknowledge this. The orca because admitting borrowed technique diminishes the ritual's mythic weight. The penguins because acknowledging their own deep-communion arts survived inside the weapon that broke their defenses is a grief no memorial liturgy is shaped to hold.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771585993903_1", anchor: "confirmed by interrogation of blood-speaker initiate Kzund-threk-zaith", text: "\"Interrogation.\" The administrative ice from the eastern shelf approaches records a juvenile orca, barely past pod-age, held in a sanctified ice-cell for nine days. I do not know what methods produced this testimony. The record is specific about what Kzund-threk-zaith said and silent about what was done to make him say it.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771585993903_2", anchor: "still produce anomalous resonance", text: "Foundation Depths, catalogue stratum 7, drawer 1140. It hums when the temperature drops. Someone carried that wing-bone home from a battlefield and someone else catalogued it and someone else filed it in a drawer where I found it, alone, in a archive with no assistants and no funding, and it was still humming. Still carrying the force that broke it. I confirmed the resonance. I wish I had not.", type: :tangent, display: :full
